---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../../components/AuthorByline.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'Medicare Part D Prescription Drug Plans Explained';
const seoTitle = 'Medicare Part D Drug Plans in New York and Connecticut';
const description = 'Medicare Part D covers prescription drugs through private plans. Learn how Part D works, what it costs, and how to avoid the late-enrollment penalty.';
const today = '2026-06-03';

const serviceSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Service', name: 'Medicare Part D Prescription Drug Plans', serviceType: 'Medicare Prescription Drug Plan', provider: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#organization` }, areaServed: [{ '@type': 'State', name: 'New York' }, { '@type': 'State', name: 'Connecticut' }], description };
const speakableSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/services/prescription-drug-plans#webpage`, name: title, speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['.lead', 'h2'] } };
const schema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@graph': [serviceSchema, speakableSchema] };
---

<BaseLayout title={title} seoTitle={seoTitle} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <PageHero
    title="Medicare Part D (Prescription Drug Plans)"
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Medicare Plans', href: '/services' }, { label: 'Part D Drug Plans' }]}
  />

  <div class="bg-crst-light border-b border-navy-100">
    <div class="container-wide py-3 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-2 sm:gap-4 text-center">
      <span class="text-sm font-heading text-navy-700">Questions about your drug coverage?</span>
      <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-sm px-5 py-2">Call or text {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="py-14 lg:py-18">
    <div class="container-narrow prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-crst-primary prose-a:text-crst-accent">

      <div class="not-prose mb-8 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-crst-gold bg-navy-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-navy-800 leading-relaxed m-0">
          <strong>Medicare Part D</strong> covers prescription drugs through private plans. You can add a standalone Part D plan to
          Original Medicare — often alongside a <a href="/services/medicare-supplement">Medigap</a> plan — or get drug coverage
          bundled inside most Medicare Advantage plans.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="lead text-xl text-navy-700">
        Part D is the piece people put off, then regret skipping — because the penalty for enrolling late is permanent. Here’s how it
        works.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-500 -mt-2">
        Written by <a href="/about">Jim Farnham, MS, MBA</a> — Licensed Agent / Insurance Broker (NY license {BUSINESS.nyLicense}).
      </p>

      <h2>How Medicare Part D works</h2>
      <p>
        Each Part D plan has a formulary — the list of drugs it covers, organized into tiers that determine your share of the cost.
        Plans differ in which drugs they cover and at what tier, so the plan that’s lowest-cost for your neighbor may not be the lowest-cost
        for you. The right plan depends on the specific medications you take.
      </p>

      <h2>What Part D costs</h2>
      <p>
        Expect a monthly premium, a possible deductible, and copays or coinsurance based on your plan’s tiers. Higher earners may pay
        an income-related amount on top of the premium. Because the variable is your own drug list, comparing total expected annual
        cost — not just the premium — is the way to choose.
      </p>

      <h2>What every plan must cover</h2>
      <p>
        According to <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/part-d/what-drug-plans-cover" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Medicare.gov</a>,
        Part D is extra coverage that Medicare-approved private plans offer, and all plans must cover a wide range of the prescription drugs
        people with Medicare take, including most drugs in certain protected classes, like drugs to treat cancer, HIV/AIDS or depression.
        Each plan has its own formulary, which is its list of covered drugs. Medicare also suggests considering drug coverage even if you
        don’t take prescription drugs now.
      </p>

      <h2>Help paying for drug costs</h2>
      <p>
        “Extra Help” is a Medicare program that helps people with limited income and resources pay Part D premiums, deductibles, coinsurance
        and other costs, and you don’t pay a Part D late-enrollment penalty while you get it. You get Extra Help automatically if you have
        full-benefit Medicaid, get help from your state paying your Part B premiums through a Medicare Savings Program, or receive
        Supplemental Security Income (SSI) payments. Otherwise you can apply. For 2026, the limits on
        <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/help/drug-costs" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Medicare.gov</a>
        are income of $23,940 and resources of $18,090 for an individual, and income of $32,460 and resources of $36,100 for a married couple.
        Limits can change each year. Your State Health Insurance Assistance Program (SHIP) can help you apply at no cost.
      </p>

      <h2>Part D with Medigap, or inside Medicare Advantage</h2>
      <p>
        A Medigap plan does not cover prescription drugs, so people who pair Original Medicare with Medigap usually add a stand-alone Part D
        plan. See <a href="/services/medicare-supplement">Medicare Supplement (Medigap)</a> and
        <a href="/what-medigap-plan-g-does-not-cover">what Plan G does not cover</a>. Most
        <a href="/services/medicare-advantage">Medicare Advantage plans</a> include drug coverage in the plan itself, so in that case you would
        not buy a separate Part D plan.
      </p>

      <h2>What to compare</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Your own medications.</strong> Check that each drug is on the plan’s formulary and note which tier it is in.</li>
        <li><strong>Your pharmacy.</strong> Plans can price the same drug differently depending on the pharmacy you use.</li>
        <li><strong>The deductible and the copay or coinsurance.</strong> The premium is only part of what you pay.</li>
        <li><strong>The total for the year.</strong> Add the premium to what you would expect to pay for your drugs, and compare plans on that number.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I can run that comparison for your medication list and your pharmacy, by phone or Zoom screen-share, at no cost to you.
      </p>

      <h2>Avoiding the late-enrollment penalty</h2>
      <p>
        If you go without creditable drug coverage for 63 days or more after your Initial Enrollment Period, Medicare can add a
        permanent penalty to your premium. Even if you take few or no medications, a low-premium plan avoids that risk — see the
        <a href="/medicare-part-d-late-enrollment-penalty">Part D late-enrollment penalty</a> guide for the details.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-white font-heading text-lg mb-3 m-0">Want help comparing Part D for your medications?</p>
        <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-base px-7 py-3">Call {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
      </div>

      <p>
        Related: <a href="/medicare-basics">Medicare basics</a> and
        <a href="/services/medicare-supplement">Medicare Supplement</a>.
      </p>

      <AuthorByline />

      <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <Disclaimer variant="tpmo" />
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          Plan costs and formularies are set by Medicare and plans and change annually. This page is educational and not
          individualized advice. NY producer license #:
          {' '}{BUSINESS.nyLicense}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection headline="Let’s find the Part D plan that fits your medications" />
</BaseLayout>
